# Line endings.
#
# This repo has core.autocrlf=true on at least one machine, which hands the
# working tree CRLF on checkout. Git itself copes — it normalises back to LF
# when staging, so a CRLF working copy and an LF one are the same commit
# (measured: identical blob hashes). Two things do NOT cope, and only those are
# pinned here rather than renormalising the whole repo.

# 1. Shell scripts. A CRLF shebang line fails on Linux and macOS, so a checkout
#    on either would ship a broken tools/tiled/install.sh and scripts/*.sh.
*.sh text eol=lf

# 2. Zone files. Both zone editors write LF by design — Tiled through BinaryFile
#    (its TextFile writes CRLF on Windows, +14602 bytes on world.json), and the
#    in-game editor through a browser download of a \n string. The byte-for-byte
#    round-trip that guards the two against each other (plan-tiled-authoring.md
#    D6) compares against the file on disk, so a CRLF checkout would report
#    ~14600 differences with nothing edited.
api/zones/*.json text eol=lf

# 3. Git hooks. Same shebang problem as the shell scripts, and the files carry
#    no .sh extension.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# 3. Localization files (plan-localization.md L16). The generated English
#    source ARBs are compared byte for byte against a fresh extraction (D19),
#    and the translated-from records the same way, so a CRLF checkout would
#    read as stale with nothing edited.
*.arb text eol=lf
api/lang/**/*.json text eol=lf
frontend/src/lang/*.json text eol=lf
